 The session cookie is also applied to error responses, so a request that throws still slides the window and still persists a session created during it.
 
+## Rotate the Password
+
+Sessions are sealed with `password`. To change it without signing everyone out, move the old password to `extraUnsealPasswords` for a while. H3 opens sessions with `password` first and then with each extra password, in order:
+
+```js
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: "f3b1c7e0-5d2a-4b8e-9a61-2c7d0e4f8b93", // the new password
+  extraUnsealPasswords: ["80d42cfb-1cd2-462c-8f17-e3237d9027e9"], // the previous one
+  maxAge: 60 * 60 * 24 * 7,
+});
+```
+
+Extra passwords are only used to open sessions. Every write uses `password`, and a cookie opened with an extra password is resealed with `password` on the same request, so active users move over without signing in again. Once the window has passed, remove the entry from the list.
+
+Keep these limits in mind while the window is open:
+
+- Keep the window longer than `maxAge` or `idleTimeout`. Sessions still sealed with the old password stay valid until the window closes, and removing the entry earlier signs them out.
+- Each extra password costs one PBKDF2 derivation for every cookie that does not open with `password`, so keep the list short.
+- Each entry must be at least 32 characters. An invalid entry throws an error that names its index, instead of silently signing users out.
+- Sessions sent through the `x-{name}-session` header cannot be resealed, so they keep depending on the extra password until it is removed.
+- Do not move the old password back into `password`. Sessions sealed with the new one stop opening until it is restored.
+
+> [!IMPORTANT]
+> Resealing writes a `Set-Cookie` on a request that only reads the session. If the first request after rotation overlaps with another request that writes the session, whichever response the browser applies last wins, so the write can be lost. This is the same caveat as `idleTimeout` above.
+
 ## Use Multiple Sessions
 
 Because each session is stored under its own `name`, you can run several independent sessions on the same request. They live in separate cookies and never overwrite each other, which is useful for keeping unrelated concerns apart, such as a long-lived auth session and a short-lived flash message:
